  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="cost">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How much does a hot water cylinder cost in St Helens?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A vented cylinder replaced starts from £750. An unvented cylinder starts from £1,450, and the price goes up with the size. All prices include VAT.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Price, inc VAT</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Vented cylinder replaced</td><td>from £750</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder, 150 litres</td><td>from £1,450</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder, 210 litres</td><td>from £1,695</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder, 250 to 300 litres</td><td>from £1,995</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Change from vented to unvented</td><td>from £1,895</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Immersion heater, vented cylinder</td><td>£175</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Immersion heater, unvented cylinder</td><td>£275</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder yearly service</td><td>£120</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Repairs and fault finding</td><td>£120 first hour (weekdays 9am to 4pm)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>What is in the price?</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>The new cylinder, supplied and fitted</li>
        <li>All pipe connections, valves and fittings</li>
        <li>Safety valves, expansion vessel and discharge pipe on unvented cylinders</li>
        <li>Testing and setting up before we leave</li>
        <li>Old cylinder drained, removed and taken away</li>
        <li>Building Regulations paperwork for unvented cylinders</li>
        <li>12-month workmanship guarantee</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Repairs out of hours are £150 for the first hour in the evening, at night and at weekends, and £180 on bank holidays. There is no call-out fee. If a job needs more time, we tell you the price before we carry on.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="terraces">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Is the old cylinder in my St Helens terrace due for replacing?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If it went in during the 1970s or 1980s, it is well past its expected life. Most cylinders last 15 to 25 years, so plan a swap before it fails.</p>
      <p>Most Victorian terraces in WA10 still have vented copper cylinders. Many were fitted when central heating first went in, 40 to 50 years ago. Plenty still work. But the risk of a sudden split goes up every year.</p>
      <p>When an old cylinder fails, the water goes fast. In a terrace, the airing cupboard is often above the kitchen. A leak can soak a ceiling and the units below before you see it.</p>
      <p>A planned swap from £750 costs far less than that damage. At the free survey, we check the age and state of your cylinder. We tell you honestly if it needs doing now or can wait.</p>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Cylinder leaking or no hot water today?</strong> Call and we will tell you what we can do. We cover every WA postcode with no call-out fee.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="immersion">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do I need a new immersion heater or a new cylinder?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If the cylinder is sound, a new immersion heater from £175 brings your hot water back. If the cylinder is old or the immersion keeps failing, a new cylinder is better value.</p>
      <p>A failed immersion heater is one of the most common calls we get from St Helens. Water in parts of St Helens is harder than in Liverpool. Scale builds up on the element, so it takes longer to heat, then stops.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>A new immersion heater is right if</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>The cylinder is under about 15 years old</li>
            <li>There is no sign of leaks or rust</li>
            <li>This is the first time the immersion has failed</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>A new cylinder is right if</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>The cylinder is over 20 years old</li>
            <li>The immersion has failed before</li>
            <li>The tank is leaking, rusty or you often run out</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">An immersion heater is £175 on a vented cylinder and £275 on an unvented one. Both are often done the same day. We will not sell you a cylinder you do not need.</p>
      <h3>Other signs to look out for</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Hot water takes much longer to heat than it used to</li>
        <li>Banging or kettling noises from the cylinder</li>
        <li>A drip from a valve or fitting, which can often be fixed</li>
        <li>Weak hot water, but strong cold water</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="bathroom">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do I need a new cylinder if I move my bathroom upstairs?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Not always. It depends on how high your loft tank is above the new shower. If the pressure will be too weak, an unvented cylinder solves it.</p>
      <p>Many WA10 terraces once had the bathroom downstairs at the back. Lots of owners now move it upstairs. That changes how your hot water works.</p>
      <p>On a vented system, the pressure comes from the height of the loft tank above the tap. An upstairs shower sits much closer to the tank. So the flow can be weak.</p>
      <p>An unvented cylinder runs at mains pressure, wherever the bathroom is. It suits a strong thermostatic shower upstairs. We test your mains pressure and flow before we suggest it.</p>
      <p>We check the hot water as part of every bathroom survey. Our <a href="/bathroom-fitters-st-helens">bathroom fitters in St Helens</a> can do the move and the cylinder under one fixed price.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="types">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Vented or unvented: which suits my St Helens home?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If your loft tank is sound and you are happy with your shower, a vented swap is the cheapest fix. For stronger showers or an extra bathroom, go unvented.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Vented cylinder</th><th>Unvented cylinder</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Fed by</strong></td><td>A cold water tank in the loft</td><td>The mains water supply</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Pressure</strong></td><td>Low, from gravity</td><td>Mains pressure</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>WA10 terraces with the loft tank in place</td><td>Upstairs bathrooms and extra bathrooms</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Who can fit it</strong></td><td>A qualified plumber</td><td>A G3 qualified engineer only</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Yearly service</strong></td><td>Not usually needed</td><td>Yes, to keep the warranty</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Price with us</strong></td><td>from £750</td><td>from £1,450</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Most St Helens homes with a system or regular boiler have an indirect cylinder. The boiler heats it through a coil, with an immersion heater as a backup. We fit vented and unvented versions.</p>
      <p>Only have one bathroom? A combi boiler may suit you better, with no tank at all. See our <a href="/boiler-installation-st-helens">boiler installation prices in St Helens</a>, from £2,195.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does a cylinder installation work?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We survey for free, give you a fixed written price, then fit the new cylinder. Most swaps are done in one day.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Free survey.</strong> We check your cylinder, your boiler and how much hot water you use. For unvented, we test your mains pressure and flow.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed written price.</strong> It covers the cylinder, the fittings, the safety parts and the paperwork.</li>
        <li><strong>Fitting day.</strong> We protect your floors, drain the old cylinder and take it out. Then we fit and connect the new one.</li>
        <li><strong>Testing.</strong> We fill the system, check for leaks and test every safety valve. We show you the controls.</li>
        <li><strong>Paperwork.</strong> Unvented work is notified as the Building Regulations require. You get the certificate and warranty details.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>Why G3 matters</h3>
      <p>An unvented cylinder holds hot water under mains pressure. Its safety valves must work every time. That is why only a qualified engineer can fit one. All our engineers are G3 qualified. Always ask for proof of G3 before anyone fits an unvented cylinder in your home.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
